window.__ch("<h1 id=\"part-14-technical-foundations-for-pms\">PART 14: TECHNICAL FOUNDATIONS FOR PMs</h1>\n<p>You don't need to write production code to be a great PM. But you do need genuine technical fluency. Without it: you can't have substantive conversations with engineers; you'll miss the trade-offs that matter; you'll make commitments you can't keep; engineers will lose respect for you.</p>\n<p>This chapter teaches the technical foundation a PM needs in 2026. It's deep enough that an engineer will respect you, broad enough to cover the ground you'll encounter daily.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-1-why-technical-fluency-matters\">14.1 Why Technical Fluency Matters</h2>\n<p>Three specific reasons technical fluency pays off:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Better trade-off decisions.</strong>\n&quot;Can we add this feature?&quot; requires knowing roughly how complex it is, what existing systems it touches, what the maintenance cost will be. Without fluency, you make commitments engineers privately mock.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Better communication with engineers.</strong>\nWhen an engineer says &quot;this requires a schema migration&quot; or &quot;the API is rate-limited,&quot; you should understand at the conceptual level what they mean. Otherwise, conversations slow.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Better product decisions about technical-flavored features.</strong>\nPerformance, reliability, security, scalability — these aren't just engineering concerns. They affect users. PMs who don't understand them ship products that fail at scale.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-bar-by-company-type\">The Bar by Company Type</h3>\n<p>The technical bar varies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B2B infra companies (Stripe, Twilio, Snowflake, Databricks, Cloudflare):</strong> Very high. PMs often have CS degrees and prior eng experience. You'll read API specs, understand distributed systems, sometimes write code.</li>\n<li><strong>Developer tools (GitHub, Vercel, Linear):</strong> High. Similar to above.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer tech (Meta, Google consumer, Pinterest, TikTok):</strong> Medium-high. Need to reason about ML systems and scale.</li>\n<li><strong>B2B SaaS (Salesforce, HubSpot, Zoom):</strong> Medium. APIs, integrations, security, compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer non-tech-first (FinTech, edtech, marketplaces):</strong> Medium. Domain knowledge often more important than tech depth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The bar is rising everywhere. AI-era PMs especially need depth.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-2-how-the-internet-works\">14.2 How the Internet Works</h2>\n<p>Foundation level: how does any web request work?</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-basic-flow\">The Basic Flow</h3>\n<p>When you type <code>instagram.com</code> in your browser:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>DNS lookup.</strong> Your computer asks DNS servers: &quot;what's the IP address for instagram.com?&quot; Returns something like <code>157.240.196.174</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>TCP connection.</strong> Your computer establishes a connection to that IP address on port 443 (HTTPS).</li>\n<li><strong>TLS handshake.</strong> Encryption is set up — your computer and Instagram's server agree on encryption keys.</li>\n<li><strong>HTTP request.</strong> Your computer sends <code>GET /</code> along with headers (cookies, user-agent, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Server processing.</strong> Instagram's servers receive the request, look up data, generate a response.</li>\n<li><strong>HTTP response.</strong> Server sends back HTML (and often JSON if API call), CSS, JavaScript, images.</li>\n<li><strong>Browser rendering.</strong> Browser parses the HTML, executes JavaScript, fetches additional resources, renders pixels.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"key-concepts-to-understand\">Key Concepts to Understand</h3>\n<p><strong>HTTP methods:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>GET</code> — read data (&quot;show me users&quot;).</li>\n<li><code>POST</code> — create data (&quot;add a user&quot;).</li>\n<li><code>PUT</code> / <code>PATCH</code> — update data.</li>\n<li><code>DELETE</code> — remove data.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Status codes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>200 OK</code> — success.</li>\n<li><code>301 / 302</code> — redirects.</li>\n<li><code>400 Bad Request</code> — client sent bad data.</li>\n<li><code>401 Unauthorized</code> — not authenticated.</li>\n<li><code>403 Forbidden</code> — authenticated but not allowed.</li>\n<li><code>404 Not Found</code> — resource doesn't exist.</li>\n<li><code>500 Internal Server Error</code> — server crashed.</li>\n<li><code>503 Service Unavailable</code> — server overloaded.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Headers</strong> — metadata about the request/response (auth tokens, content-type, caching directives).</p>\n<p><strong>Cookies</strong> — small bits of data the server stores in your browser; used for sessions, tracking.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-this-matters\">Why This Matters</h3>\n<p>When users say &quot;the page is slow,&quot; PMs who understand the request flow can ask the right questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is DNS slow?</li>\n<li>Is the server slow to respond?</li>\n<li>Are we sending too much data?</li>\n<li>Is the browser slow to render?</li>\n<li>Is there a redirect chain?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each of those has different solutions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-3-apis-explained\">14.3 APIs Explained</h2>\n<p>APIs are how systems talk to each other. Most modern products consume and expose APIs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-an-api-is\">What an API Is</h3>\n<p>A contract between two systems: &quot;if you send a request shaped like this, I'll respond shaped like that.&quot;</p>\n<p>Example (Stripe's API):</p>\n<pre><code>POST /v1/charges\nAuthorization: Bearer sk_test_xxxxx\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  &quot;amount&quot;: 50000,\n  &quot;currency&quot;: &quot;inr&quot;,\n  &quot;source&quot;: &quot;tok_xxx&quot;,\n  &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Order #1234&quot;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Returns:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;ch_1234567&quot;,\n  &quot;status&quot;: &quot;succeeded&quot;,\n  &quot;amount&quot;: 50000,\n  ...\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The API spec defines the request format, the response format, the authentication, and the error handling.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rest-apis\">REST APIs</h3>\n<p>The dominant API style. Built around resources (nouns) and HTTP methods.</p>\n<pre><code>GET    /users        → list users\nGET    /users/123    → get user 123\nPOST   /users        → create user\nPUT    /users/123    → update user 123\nDELETE /users/123    → delete user 123\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Simple, ubiquitous, well-tooled.\n<strong>Weaknesses:</strong> Multiple round trips for complex data; over-fetching; under-fetching.</p>\n<h3 id=\"graphql\">GraphQL</h3>\n<p>Alternative API style. Single endpoint; clients specify what they want.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-graphql\">query {\n  user(id: 123) {\n    name\n    email\n    orders(last: 5) {\n      id\n      total\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Clients get exactly what they need; flexible; one endpoint.\n<strong>Weaknesses:</strong> More server complexity; harder to cache; learning curve.</p>\n<p>Used by Facebook, GitHub, Shopify, and others. PMs at companies using GraphQL should know the basics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"webhooks\">Webhooks</h3>\n<p>Reverse APIs. Instead of you polling for changes, the system calls <em>you</em> when something happens.</p>\n<pre><code>You register a webhook URL with Stripe.\nWhen a payment succeeds, Stripe POSTs to your URL with the event data.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Webhooks are critical for real-time integrations. Common pitfalls:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Webhooks can fail; design for retries.</li>\n<li><strong>Security:</strong> Webhooks must be signed/verified to prevent spoofing.</li>\n<li><strong>Idempotency:</strong> Same event might arrive twice; handle gracefully.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"grpc\">gRPC</h3>\n<p>Modern RPC framework. Fast, strongly typed, used heavily for service-to-service in microservices.</p>\n<p>PMs encounter gRPC mostly in B2B infrastructure contexts. Most product PMs don't need to go deep here.</p>\n<h3 id=\"api-design-what-pms-should-know\">API Design — What PMs Should Know</h3>\n<p>When designing or reviewing an API:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Resource hierarchy clear.</strong> <code>/users/{id}/orders/{id}</code> is intuitive; <code>/getUserOrders?userId=X</code> less so.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Naming consistent.</strong> Pluralization (<code>/users</code> not <code>/user</code>), conventions on naming.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Versioning.</strong> APIs evolve; you need a strategy. <code>/v1/</code>, <code>/v2/</code> URLs is one approach.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Pagination.</strong> Lists must be paginated. Cursor-based or offset-based; cursor is generally better.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Errors that mean something.</strong> Not just <code>400</code>; explain what went wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Rate limits.</strong> Document them; return clear errors when hit.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Authentication.</strong> API keys (simple), OAuth (for user-context APIs), JWT, mTLS (high-security).</p>\n<p><strong>8. Idempotency.</strong> A repeated request shouldn't double-charge a customer. Idempotency keys.</p>\n<p>PMs at companies whose API is the product (Stripe, Twilio, Algolia) live in these decisions daily.</p>\n<h3 id=\"api-as-product\">API as Product</h3>\n<p>For developer-facing companies, the API is the product. PMs there think about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Developer experience</strong> (DX) at every step.</li>\n<li><strong>Documentation as a first-class deliverable.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Onboarding flow</strong> (signup → first API call in &lt;5 min).</li>\n<li><strong>Error message quality.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>SDKs</strong> in major languages.</li>\n<li><strong>Sandbox / test mode.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stripe is the canonical example of API-as-product done right.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-4-databases\">14.4 Databases</h2>\n<p>How data is stored and queried. PMs benefit from foundational understanding.</p>\n<h3 id=\"two-major-categories\">Two Major Categories</h3>\n<p><strong>Relational (SQL) databases.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Examples: Postgres, MySQL, SQLite, Snowflake, BigQuery.</li>\n<li>Data in tables (rows and columns) with strict schema.</li>\n<li>SQL for queries.</li>\n<li>Strong consistency, transactions, joins.</li>\n<li>Best for: structured data, financial transactions, business apps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>NoSQL databases.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Document (MongoDB, DynamoDB, Firestore): JSON-like documents.</li>\n<li>Key-value (Redis): simple key→value lookups, in-memory.</li>\n<li>Wide-column (Cassandra): for massive scale, time-series.</li>\n<li>Graph (Neo4j): for connected data.</li>\n<li>Best for: specific workloads (caching, time-series, hierarchies, scale-out).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most products use a mix: Postgres for primary data, Redis for caching, search engines (Elasticsearch) for text search.</p>\n<h3 id=\"schema-and-indexing\">Schema and Indexing</h3>\n<p><strong>Schema:</strong> The structure of your data. In SQL, you define tables with columns and types. Schema changes are expensive — adding a column to a 1B-row table can take hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Indexes:</strong> Pre-computed structures that make queries fast. Without an index on <code>users.email</code>, finding a user by email requires scanning every row. With an index, it's instant.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Indexes speed reads but slow writes. Adding too many indexes makes inserts/updates slow.</p>\n<p>This explains why &quot;just add a filter on this field&quot; can take 2 weeks: it might require an index that affects write performance, schema changes that require migration, and so on. PMs who understand this don't make naive estimates.</p>\n<h3 id=\"transactions\">Transactions</h3>\n<p>A transaction is a group of operations that succeed or fail together.</p>\n<pre><code>BEGIN;\n  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE user_id = 1;\n  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE user_id = 2;\nCOMMIT;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Either both updates happen or neither. Critical for financial operations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"acid-vs-base\">ACID vs BASE</h3>\n<p><strong>ACID</strong> (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) — the guarantees relational databases provide.</p>\n<p><strong>BASE</strong> (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent) — the relaxed guarantees of many NoSQL systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> ACID systems are easier to reason about but harder to scale. BASE systems scale better but require careful design (you might read stale data).</p>\n<p>For a payments system: ACID is essential. For a &quot;likes&quot; counter: eventual consistency is fine.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-to-migrate-databases\">When to Migrate Databases</h3>\n<p>PMs sometimes face &quot;should we migrate from X to Y?&quot; decisions. Migrations are expensive (months) and risky. Common drivers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Performance ceiling on current DB.</li>\n<li>Cost of current DB at scale.</li>\n<li>Specific features needed.</li>\n<li>Operational burden.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most PMs should default to: avoid database migrations unless absolutely necessary; talk to senior engineers before considering.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-5-system-architecture\">14.5 System Architecture</h2>\n<p>How software systems are structured. PMs need conceptual fluency.</p>\n<h3 id=\"monolith-vs-microservices\">Monolith vs Microservices</h3>\n<p><strong>Monolith:</strong> One big codebase deployed as one unit.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pros: Simpler operations; easier debugging; faster development at small scale.</li>\n<li>Cons: Slower deploys at scale; one bug can take down everything; team scaling friction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Microservices:</strong> Many small services, each independently deployed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pros: Teams ship independently; failures isolated; tech-stack flexibility.</li>\n<li>Cons: Operational complexity; distributed systems issues (networking, consistency); harder debugging.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most companies start with monolith and split into services as they grow. Premature microservices is one of the most common engineering mistakes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-architectural-components\">Common Architectural Components</h3>\n<p><strong>Load balancer.</strong>\nDistributes incoming requests across multiple servers. So one server doesn't get overwhelmed.</p>\n<p><strong>CDN (Content Delivery Network).</strong>\nCaches static content (images, CSS, JS) at locations near users. Cloudflare, Fastly, Akamai. Speeds up content delivery globally.</p>\n<p><strong>Cache.</strong>\nMemory-fast storage for frequently accessed data. Redis, Memcached. Reduces database load.</p>\n<p><strong>Queue / message broker.</strong>\nFor async work. Producer puts jobs on queue; consumer processes them. RabbitMQ, Kafka, AWS SQS.</p>\n<p><strong>Search engine.</strong>\nSpecialized for text search. Elasticsearch, Algolia, Meilisearch.</p>\n<p><strong>Object storage.</strong>\nFiles (images, videos, PDFs). S3, GCS, Azure Blob.</p>\n<p><strong>Database.</strong>\nPrimary data store. Postgres, MySQL, etc.</p>\n<p>A typical web application:</p>\n<pre><code>[CDN] ← serves static content\n   ↓\n[Load Balancer]\n   ↓\n[Web Servers] ← run application code\n   ↓ ↓ ↓\n[Cache (Redis)]  [Database (Postgres)]  [Queue (Kafka)]  [Object storage (S3)]\n                                                  ↓\n                                          [Background workers]\n</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"mobile-vs-web-architecture\">Mobile vs Web Architecture</h3>\n<p><strong>Web:</strong> Code runs on browser; server provides data. Network round-trips affect everything.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HTML / CSS / JavaScript on client.</li>\n<li>React, Vue, Svelte are popular UI frameworks.</li>\n<li>Rendering modes: client-side (CSR), server-side (SSR), static (SSG).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mobile:</strong> Code runs as native app; client has more state.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>iOS: Swift / SwiftUI.</li>\n<li>Android: Kotlin / Jetpack Compose.</li>\n<li>Cross-platform: React Native, Flutter — write once, run on both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mobile development cycles are slower because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>App store review (1-2 days at minimum).</li>\n<li>Users may not update; you support old versions.</li>\n<li>Testing across many devices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"async-vs-sync\">Async vs Sync</h3>\n<p><strong>Sync:</strong> Caller waits for response. Simple. Slow if anything takes time.\n<strong>Async:</strong> Caller submits request and continues; response comes later (callback, promise, queue).</p>\n<p>For user-facing operations: short ones sync, long ones async.</p>\n<p>Example: User uploads a photo. The upload itself can be sync (user sees progress). Image processing (resizing, ML tagging) happens async — user gets notification when done.</p>\n<p>PMs designing flows should think: which operations need to be fast (sync)? Which can be async without user noticing? Which are async but need clear user communication?</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-6-cloud-basics\">14.6 Cloud Basics</h2>\n<p>Most modern infrastructure runs on cloud providers. PMs should know the basics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-three\">The Big Three</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AWS (Amazon Web Services):</strong> Largest, most mature.</li>\n<li><strong>GCP (Google Cloud):</strong> Strong for data and ML.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure (Microsoft):</strong> Strong in enterprise.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"common-services\">Common Services</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compute:</strong> EC2 (AWS), Compute Engine (GCP), VMs (Azure). Virtual machines.</li>\n<li><strong>Container orchestration:</strong> ECS / EKS (AWS), GKE (GCP), AKS (Azure). Run containers (Docker, Kubernetes).</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless:</strong> Lambda (AWS), Cloud Functions (GCP), Azure Functions. Code that runs in response to events.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage:</strong> S3 / GCS / Blob.</li>\n<li><strong>Databases:</strong> RDS, BigQuery, Cosmos DB.</li>\n<li><strong>CDN:</strong> CloudFront, Cloud CDN.</li>\n<li><strong>AI/ML:</strong> Bedrock (AWS), Vertex AI (GCP), Azure OpenAI.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-pms-need-to-know\">What PMs Need to Know</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud spend can be 10-30% of revenue for some companies. Optimization matters.</li>\n<li>Multi-cloud is sometimes strategic (avoid lock-in) but expensive operationally.</li>\n<li>Specific compliance and data residency requirements (e.g., data must stay in India for some financial products).</li>\n<li>The shift from on-prem to cloud is mostly complete; the next shift is &quot;edge&quot; (compute closer to users).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-7-the-deployment-pipeline\">14.7 The Deployment Pipeline</h2>\n<p>How code goes from a developer's laptop to production users.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stages\">Stages</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Code review.</strong> Engineer submits a Pull Request (PR); colleagues review and approve.</li>\n<li><strong>CI (Continuous Integration).</strong> Automated tests run on the PR. Code is merged only if tests pass.</li>\n<li><strong>Staging.</strong> Code deployed to a staging environment that mirrors production. QA happens here.</li>\n<li><strong>Production deploy.</strong> Code rolls out to production users. Often gradually (1% → 10% → 100%).</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring.</strong> Errors, latency, usage tracked. Issues trigger alerts.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"modern-deployment-practices\">Modern Deployment Practices</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Continuous Deployment (CD):</strong> Code is auto-deployed to production after passing tests. Many web companies do this multiple times a day.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature flags:</strong> Decouple &quot;deployed code&quot; from &quot;active feature.&quot; Code can be in production but turned off until ready.</li>\n<li><strong>Canary deployments:</strong> Roll out to a small % first, monitor, then expand.</li>\n<li><strong>Blue-green deployments:</strong> Run two production environments; switch traffic once new version is verified.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PMs benefit from understanding this because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Speed of shipping.</strong> Modern teams can deploy 50× a day.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk management.</strong> Feature flags reduce launch risk dramatically.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollback.</strong> When something breaks, fast rollback matters.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-8-performance-and-scalability\">14.8 Performance and Scalability</h2>\n<p>How fast can your product respond, and how many users can it handle?</p>\n<h3 id=\"performance-concepts\">Performance Concepts</h3>\n<p><strong>Latency:</strong> Time for a single request to complete. Measured in milliseconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Throughput:</strong> How many requests per second the system can handle.</p>\n<p><strong>P50 / P95 / P99:</strong> Percentile latencies. P99 = 99% of requests are faster than this. The P99 is what bad-day users experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Bandwidth:</strong> How much data can move through a network per second.</p>\n<p><strong>Doherty Threshold:</strong> ~400ms perceived as &quot;instant.&quot; Beyond, users feel friction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"scaling-strategies\">Scaling Strategies</h3>\n<p><strong>Vertical scaling:</strong> Bigger machines. Easy; has limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Horizontal scaling:</strong> More machines. Requires the system to be designed for it (stateless services, distributed databases).</p>\n<p><strong>Caching:</strong> Faster than computing. Cache as much as possible; invalidate carefully.</p>\n<p><strong>Async processing:</strong> Move slow work off the request path. User clicks; you respond fast; actual work happens in background.</p>\n<p><strong>CDN / edge:</strong> Push content closer to users. India users hitting a US server have ~250ms baseline latency before any compute.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-breaks-at-scale\">What Breaks at Scale</h3>\n<p>A common pattern: products work great at 10K users, struggle at 100K, completely break at 1M. Common breaking points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Database overload:</strong> Queries that work for small datasets become unbearably slow.</li>\n<li><strong>Single points of failure:</strong> Components that work alone fail when traffic 10×s.</li>\n<li><strong>Cascading failures:</strong> Slow downstream service slows everything; cascades.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory leaks</strong> that accumulate over hours and crash production.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PMs should celebrate engineering investments in scale-out infrastructure even when they &quot;look like&quot; they don't add user value. They prevent painful crashes and slow times that <em>do</em> affect users.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-9-reliability-uptime-slas-error-budgets\">14.9 Reliability — Uptime, SLAs, Error Budgets</h2>\n<p>Reliability is a feature. Users notice when products break.</p>\n<h3 id=\"uptime\">Uptime</h3>\n<p>Measured as a percentage. Common targets:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>99% uptime = 3.65 days/year of downtime.</li>\n<li>99.9% uptime = 8.76 hours/year.</li>\n<li>99.99% uptime = 52.6 minutes/year.</li>\n<li>99.999% uptime = 5.26 minutes/year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each &quot;9&quot; added is roughly 10× more expensive in engineering and infrastructure investment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"slas-service-level-agreements\">SLAs (Service Level Agreements)</h3>\n<p>Promises made to customers about uptime, latency, support response. B2B contracts often include these. PMs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Define what's promised.</li>\n<li>Track whether you're meeting it.</li>\n<li>Negotiate exceptions for legitimate reasons (planned maintenance, force majeure).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"error-budgets\">Error Budgets</h3>\n<p>Google's SRE framework: if you commit to 99.9% uptime, you have a budget of 0.1% for failures (~8.76 hours/year). When you've exhausted the budget, you must focus on stability over features.</p>\n<p>This creates healthy tension between PM (wants features) and SRE/eng (wants stability). PMs respecting error budgets is a sign of maturity.</p>\n<h3 id=\"designing-for-reliability\">Designing for Reliability</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Redundancy.</strong> Multiple servers, multiple data centers.</li>\n<li><strong>Graceful degradation.</strong> When a component fails, the rest still works (maybe with reduced functionality).</li>\n<li><strong>Health checks and auto-recovery.</strong> Failing components are restarted automatically.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring and alerting.</strong> Detect issues fast.</li>\n<li><strong>Postmortems.</strong> When things break, learn structurally.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-10-security-basics-for-pms\">14.10 Security Basics for PMs</h2>\n<p>Security is a feature users assume; its absence is catastrophic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"core-concepts\">Core Concepts</h3>\n<p><strong>Authentication.</strong> Verifying who someone is. Passwords, magic links, OAuth, biometrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Authorization.</strong> Verifying what they're allowed to do. Roles, permissions.</p>\n<p><strong>Encryption at rest.</strong> Data stored encrypted (databases, backups).</p>\n<p><strong>Encryption in transit.</strong> Data encrypted when moving over network (TLS / HTTPS).</p>\n<p><strong>MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication).</strong> Multiple proofs of identity (password + phone code).</p>\n<p><strong>Audit logs.</strong> Records of who did what when.</p>\n<h3 id=\"owasp-top-10\">OWASP Top 10</h3>\n<p>The most common security vulnerabilities. Every PM should know these conceptually:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Broken access control.</strong> Users accessing things they shouldn't.</li>\n<li><strong>Cryptographic failures.</strong> Sensitive data exposed.</li>\n<li><strong>Injection</strong> (SQL injection, command injection). Untrusted input used in queries.</li>\n<li><strong>Insecure design.</strong> Architectural mistakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Security misconfiguration.</strong> Default passwords, exposed admin panels.</li>\n<li><strong>Vulnerable components.</strong> Old libraries with known issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication failures.</strong> Weak passwords, session hijacking.</li>\n<li><strong>Software / data integrity failures.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Logging / monitoring failures.</strong> Can't detect attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Server-side request forgery (SSRF).</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>PMs don't fix these; engineers do. PMs ensure security work is prioritized.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-to-bring-security-in\">When to Bring Security In</h3>\n<p>Always. Especially:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Any new authentication / authorization design.</li>\n<li>Any feature handling sensitive data (PII, payments, health, financial).</li>\n<li>Any third-party integration.</li>\n<li>Any new data collection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Late security review is the #2 cause of preventable launch delays (after legal). Bring them in at PRD stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-11-privacy-and-data-handling\">14.11 Privacy and Data Handling</h2>\n<p>Increasingly regulated. PMs need real fluency.</p>\n<h3 id=\"major-regulations\">Major Regulations</h3>\n<p><strong>GDPR (EU):</strong> Comprehensive. Users have rights to access, delete, port their data. Heavy fines (up to 4% of global revenue).</p>\n<p><strong>CCPA / CPRA (California):</strong> Similar to GDPR. Right to know, right to delete, right to opt out of sale.</p>\n<p><strong>India DPDP Act (2023):</strong> India's privacy law. Came into effect. Consent-based; data fiduciaries (companies) have obligations. Penalties up to ₹250 Cr.</p>\n<p><strong>HIPAA (US health):</strong> For health data.</p>\n<p><strong>PCI DSS:</strong> For payment data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"privacy-principles\">Privacy Principles</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose limitation.</strong> Use data only for the purposes you collected it for.</p>\n<p><strong>Data minimization.</strong> Collect only what you need.</p>\n<p><strong>Storage limitation.</strong> Don't keep data forever.</p>\n<p><strong>User rights.</strong> Users can access, correct, delete, port their data.</p>\n<p><strong>Consent.</strong> Often required, especially for sensitive data and in regulated regions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"privacy-by-design\">Privacy by Design</h3>\n<p>Build privacy in from the start, not retrofit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Default to minimum data collection.</li>\n<li>Clear in-product privacy controls.</li>\n<li>Automatic data deletion after retention period.</li>\n<li>Anonymization / pseudonymization where possible.</li>\n<li>Privacy review as part of PRD process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PMs at companies handling personal data should treat privacy as a product feature, not a compliance burden. Users increasingly value it; brands lose trust when breached.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-12-mobile-platforms-ios-and-android\">14.12 Mobile Platforms — iOS and Android</h2>\n<p>Most consumer products are mobile-first. PMs need platform fluency.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ios-specifics\">iOS Specifics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Apple App Store. Strict review (1-2 days). Apple takes 30% (15% for small developers).</li>\n<li><strong>Languages:</strong> Swift (modern), Objective-C (legacy).</li>\n<li><strong>UI framework:</strong> SwiftUI (modern), UIKit (mature).</li>\n<li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Consistent hardware, predictable performance, design language.</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints:</strong> Strict guidelines; no sideloading (mostly); App Store rejection risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"android-specifics\">Android Specifics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Google Play Store, plus alternative stores (Samsung Galaxy Store, Huawei AppGallery, etc.). Less strict review; sideloading allowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Languages:</strong> Kotlin (modern), Java (legacy).</li>\n<li><strong>UI framework:</strong> Jetpack Compose (modern), XML layouts (mature).</li>\n<li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Massive market in India and emerging markets; flexibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints:</strong> Device fragmentation (thousands of phones); diverse OS versions; performance variance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"cross-platform\">Cross-Platform</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>React Native:</strong> JavaScript / React. Used by Meta, Shopify, Discord. Decent for most products; not for graphic-heavy.</li>\n<li><strong>Flutter:</strong> Google's framework, Dart language. Strong UI; growing adoption.</li>\n<li><strong>Native:</strong> Best performance and platform integration. Most large consumer apps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose based on team skills, performance needs, and platform features needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"indian-context-critical\">Indian Context — Critical</h3>\n<p>For Indian users specifically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Massive Android dominance</strong> (~95%).</li>\n<li><strong>Low-end devices common</strong> — design for 2GB RAM, 32GB storage.</li>\n<li><strong>App size matters</strong> — large apps don't get installed; users uninstall to save space.</li>\n<li><strong>Connectivity variable</strong> — design for 3G, intermittent connectivity.</li>\n<li><strong>Background data savings</strong> — let users control video quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Update reluctance</strong> — users don't update apps; support old versions longer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PMs for India who don't account for these realities ship products that fail in actual use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-13-web-platforms\">14.13 Web Platforms</h2>\n<p>Web is still vast; PMs working on web products need basics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"browsers-and-standards\">Browsers and Standards</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chrome dominant</strong> (~65% globally). Safari (iOS), Firefox, Edge make up the rest.</li>\n<li><strong>Web standards:</strong> HTML, CSS, JavaScript. Standardized but with minor browser differences.</li>\n<li><strong>Modern JavaScript ecosystem:</strong> React, Vue, Angular, Svelte for frameworks. Webpack/Vite for builds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"progressive-web-apps-pwas\">Progressive Web Apps (PWAs)</h3>\n<p>Web apps that behave like native apps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Install to home screen.</li>\n<li>Work offline.</li>\n<li>Push notifications.</li>\n<li>Background sync.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Used heavily in markets where native app size is a problem (India). Examples: Twitter Lite, Pinterest PWA, OLA Lite.</p>\n<h3 id=\"server-side-rendering-vs-client-side\">Server-Side Rendering vs Client-Side</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SSR:</strong> Server generates HTML. Faster first paint. Better SEO.</li>\n<li><strong>CSR:</strong> Client (browser) renders. Snappier interactions after load. Worse first-load.</li>\n<li><strong>SSG (Static Site Generation):</strong> HTML pre-generated. Fastest. Good for content sites.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Modern frameworks (Next.js, Remix) blur these lines.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-14-tech-debt-what-it-is-when-to-invest\">14.14 Tech Debt — What It Is, When to Invest</h2>\n<p>Technical debt is the accumulated cost of choosing easy short-term solutions over robust long-term ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-tech-debt-happens\">Why Tech Debt Happens</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Speed pressure.</strong> Ship now, fix later.</li>\n<li><strong>Learning over time.</strong> What seemed right then is wrong now.</li>\n<li><strong>Scaling pressure.</strong> Code that worked at 10K users breaks at 1M.</li>\n<li><strong>Departed engineers.</strong> Knowledge leaves with people.</li>\n<li><strong>Library updates.</strong> Dependencies become outdated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-cost-of-tech-debt\">The Cost of Tech Debt</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slower development.</strong> New features take longer to build.</li>\n<li><strong>More bugs.</strong> Brittle code breaks in unexpected ways.</li>\n<li><strong>Engineer turnover.</strong> Talented engineers won't work in messy codebases.</li>\n<li><strong>Reliability problems.</strong> Systems fail more often.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"when-to-invest-in-paying-down\">When to Invest in Paying Down</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>When a workflow is slowed by 50%+ due to debt.</li>\n<li>When debt blocks specific feature work you want to do.</li>\n<li>When recruiting is hurting because of code quality.</li>\n<li>When reliability is impacted.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A common approach: dedicate ~20% of engineering capacity to ongoing tech debt and infrastructure. Don't let it build to a crisis.</p>\n<p>PMs who treat tech debt as the engineering team's problem damage their relationship and the product. Fund it explicitly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-15-build-vs-buy-vs-partner\">14.15 Build vs Buy vs Partner</h2>\n<p>Every component of your product is a build/buy/partner decision.</p>\n<h3 id=\"build\">Build</h3>\n<p>Make it yourself.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pros:</strong> Full control, no per-seat fees, fits your needs exactly.</li>\n<li><strong>Cons:</strong> High initial cost, ongoing maintenance, distraction from core.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"buy\">Buy</h3>\n<p>License or subscribe to existing software.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pros:</strong> Fast time-to-market, vendor handles maintenance, vendor expertise.</li>\n<li><strong>Cons:</strong> Per-seat fees grow with you, vendor lock-in, may not fit perfectly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"partner\">Partner</h3>\n<p>Integrate with another company's product.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pros:</strong> Borrow their distribution and capability.</li>\n<li><strong>Cons:</strong> Not your IP; partner can change terms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"decision-framework\">Decision Framework</h3>\n<p>Default: <strong>buy</strong>. Build only if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Core to your differentiation (this is what you're best at).</li>\n<li>Vendor solutions don't meet your needs.</li>\n<li>Custom requirements demand it.</li>\n<li>Cost-benefit analysis favors building (rare).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common mistakes: building things that aren't core (vanity engineering), buying core capabilities (commoditizing yourself), partnering with companies that may compete with you.</p>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-should-you-build-your-own-auth\">Worked Example: Should You Build Your Own Auth?</h3>\n<p><strong>Buy:</strong> Auth0, Clerk, Stytch, Supabase Auth. Off-the-shelf, secure, scales.</p>\n<p><strong>Build:</strong> Custom auth implementation.</p>\n<p>For 99% of products: <strong>buy</strong>. Auth is hard to do well; security mistakes are catastrophic; vendors do this for a living.</p>\n<p>For products where auth is core (a security company, an identity provider): <strong>build</strong>.</p>\n<p>The decision is rarely close. PMs who insist on building auth &quot;to save money&quot; usually regret it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-16-estimating-engineering-effort-without-being-an-engineer\">14.16 Estimating Engineering Effort (Without Being an Engineer)</h2>\n<p>You don't write code, but you'll be asked &quot;how long will this take?&quot; constantly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"don-t-estimate-ask\">Don't Estimate; Ask</h3>\n<p><strong>Wrong:</strong> &quot;I think this takes 2 weeks.&quot;\n<strong>Right:</strong> &quot;What would it take to build this? What are the unknowns? What's the rough range?&quot;</p>\n<p>You're not an engineer; estimating without input is asking to be wrong.</p>\n<h3 id=\"build-pattern-recognition\">Build Pattern Recognition</h3>\n<p>Over time, you'll develop intuition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>New screen with existing data: usually quick (1-2 weeks).</li>\n<li>New screen requiring new data: medium (2-4 weeks).</li>\n<li>Feature requiring schema migration: longer (4-8 weeks).</li>\n<li>Cross-team dependencies: significantly longer.</li>\n<li>Integration with external system: highly variable, often longer than expected.</li>\n<li>AI/ML: very high uncertainty.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"calibrate-by-watching\">Calibrate by Watching</h3>\n<p>Track estimates vs actuals for your team. Most teams over-promise by 30-50% consistently. Adjust accordingly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-do-when-you-disagree\">What to Do When You Disagree</h3>\n<p>If an engineer estimates 8 weeks for what feels like 4:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Don't say &quot;that's too long.&quot;</strong> Defensive.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask: &quot;What's driving the time? What if we cut [X]?&quot;</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Discuss trade-offs.</strong> Often there's a 2-week version and an 8-week version of the same feature.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 2-week version may not be perfect, but it might be enough to test the hypothesis. The 8-week version may be over-engineered.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-17-talking-to-engineers-confidently\">14.17 Talking to Engineers Confidently</h2>\n<p>Practical patterns for productive conversations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"show-curiosity-not-authority\">Show Curiosity, Not Authority</h3>\n<p>&quot;Why does this approach take longer?&quot; beats &quot;Are you sure that's necessary?&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"use-their-language\">Use Their Language</h3>\n<p>Learn 50 words: API, schema, migration, latency, caching, rate limit, dependency, refactor, deploy, feature flag, etc. Use them properly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ask-about-trade-offs\">Ask About Trade-offs</h3>\n<p>&quot;What's the trade-off if we do X?&quot; treats engineering as collaborative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"read-some-code\">Read Some Code</h3>\n<p>You don't need to write it; reading it builds intuition. Skim PRs, especially ones touching features you own.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bring-context\">Bring Context</h3>\n<p>Engineers care about the user/business problem. Walk them through it. They'll often propose better technical solutions if they understand the problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"defend-their-work-to-others\">Defend Their Work to Others</h3>\n<p>When sales or executives ask &quot;why is this taking so long?&quot;, you defend the team's reasoning. Engineers notice and remember.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-18-reading-technical-design-docs\">14.18 Reading Technical Design Docs</h2>\n<p>Engineering design docs are how engineers communicate technical decisions. PMs should read them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-s-usually-inside\">What's Usually Inside</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Context / problem.</strong> Why this work?</li>\n<li><strong>Goals / non-goals.</strong> What we're trying to achieve.</li>\n<li><strong>Proposed design.</strong> How we'll build it.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternatives considered.</strong> What else we considered and why we rejected.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Pros and cons.</li>\n<li><strong>Open questions.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Timeline / milestones.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"how-pms-should-engage\">How PMs Should Engage</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read it.</strong> Don't skim.</li>\n<li><strong>Comment on user-impact aspects.</strong> Not architecture.</li>\n<li><strong>Push back on long timelines</strong> with curiosity.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask about edge cases and failure modes.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Approve clearly</strong> when ready.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dictating technical choices.</li>\n<li>Skipping doc and finding out at standup.</li>\n<li>Approving without reading.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-19-common-technical-trade-offs-pms-face\">14.19 Common Technical Trade-offs PMs Face</h2>\n<p>A reference table of trade-offs you'll encounter:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Trade-off</th>\n  <th>When to lean toward A</th>\n  <th>When to lean toward B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Build vs buy</td>\n  <td>Core to differentiation</td>\n  <td>Anything else</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Monolith vs microservices</td>\n  <td>Small team, early product</td>\n  <td>Scaling team and complexity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>SQL vs NoSQL</td>\n  <td>Structured data, transactions</td>\n  <td>Schema flexibility, scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Sync vs async</td>\n  <td>Fast operations, user waiting</td>\n  <td>Slow operations, user can wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Generic solution vs specific</td>\n  <td>Broad use cases</td>\n  <td>Specific high-value use case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Quality vs velocity</td>\n  <td>Mature product, high stakes</td>\n  <td>Pre-PMF, learning fast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Vendor lock-in vs flexibility</td>\n  <td>Mature, stable vendor</td>\n  <td>Strategic capability, switching risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>In-house infra vs cloud</td>\n  <td>Massive scale, specific needs</td>\n  <td>Almost everything else</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Open-source vs commercial</td>\n  <td>Engineering capacity, customization needs</td>\n  <td>Speed to market</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"14-20-ai-and-ml-for-pms-cross-reference-part-11\">14.20 AI and ML for PMs (Cross-reference Part 11)</h2>\n<p>A brief reference. Detailed treatment is in Part 11.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-ml-engineers-actually-do\">What ML Engineers Actually Do</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Train models on data (training).</li>\n<li>Evaluate model quality (eval).</li>\n<li>Deploy models to production (inference).</li>\n<li>Monitor and update over time (MLOps).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"vocabulary\">Vocabulary</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Model.</strong> The trained ML system.</li>\n<li><strong>Training data.</strong> Data used to train.</li>\n<li><strong>Inference.</strong> Running the model on new data.</li>\n<li><strong>Loss / accuracy / F1.</strong> Metrics for model quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperparameters.</strong> Knobs you tune during training.</li>\n<li><strong>Embedding.</strong> Vector representation of input.</li>\n<li><strong>Fine-tuning.</strong> Specializing a base model for your task.</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt engineering.</strong> Designing inputs to LLMs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-pms-should-know\">What PMs Should Know</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Models are statistical; they're wrong sometimes.</li>\n<li>Eval is critical (and underdone).</li>\n<li>Cost / latency / quality trade-off.</li>\n<li>Training data quality matters more than model architecture often.</li>\n<li>Production ML is harder than research ML — drift, monitoring, retraining.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-21-sql-a-pm-reference-extension-of-part-7\">14.21 SQL — A PM Reference (Extension of Part 7)</h2>\n<p>(Part 7 covered SQL basics. This extends with more practical patterns.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"window-functions\">Window Functions</h3>\n<p>For analyses across rows:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- For each user, calculate their cumulative spend over time\nSELECT\n  user_id,\n  transaction_date,\n  amount,\n  SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY transaction_date) AS cumulative_spend\nFROM transactions\nORDER BY user_id, transaction_date;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- For each user, find the time gap between transactions\nSELECT\n  user_id,\n  transaction_date,\n  transaction_date - LAG(transaction_date) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY transaction_date) AS days_since_prev\nFROM transactions;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Window functions are powerful for retention, cohort, and time-series analyses.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ctes-for-complex-queries\">CTEs for Complex Queries</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH \n  daily_active AS (\n    SELECT user_id, DATE(event_time) AS day\n    FROM events\n    GROUP BY user_id, DATE(event_time)\n  ),\n  user_first_day AS (\n    SELECT user_id, MIN(day) AS first_day\n    FROM daily_active\n    GROUP BY user_id\n  )\nSELECT\n  ufd.first_day AS cohort_day,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT ufd.user_id) AS cohort_size,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN da.day = ufd.first_day + 7 THEN da.user_id END) AS day_7_returned\nFROM user_first_day ufd\nLEFT JOIN daily_active da ON da.user_id = ufd.user_id\nGROUP BY ufd.first_day\nORDER BY ufd.first_day;\n</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"common-patterns-to-memorize\">Common Patterns to Memorize</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Funnel conversion rates.</li>\n<li>Cohort retention.</li>\n<li>Time-to-event (signup to first purchase).</li>\n<li>Top-N per group (top 10 customers per region).</li>\n<li>Period-over-period growth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These cover ~80% of PM analytical SQL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-22-practice-exercises\">14.22 Practice Exercises</h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-1-api-design-review\">Exercise 14.1 — API Design Review</h3>\n<p>Given an API specification (search &quot;Twitter API&quot; or &quot;Stripe API&quot;), critique it: what's clear, what's confusing, what would you change?</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-2-architecture-diagram\">Exercise 14.2 — Architecture Diagram</h3>\n<p>For a hypothetical product (e.g., a mobile food delivery app), draw a system architecture: clients, servers, databases, external services. Identify potential bottlenecks.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-3-build-vs-buy-decision\">Exercise 14.3 — Build vs Buy Decision</h3>\n<p>For each of these, decide build/buy/partner with rationale:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Authentication for a fintech app.</li>\n<li>AI-powered customer support for a SaaS product.</li>\n<li>Push notification system for a consumer app.</li>\n<li>Search engine for a marketplace.</li>\n<li>Analytics dashboard for end customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-4-diagnose-a-slow-page\">Exercise 14.4 — Diagnose a Slow Page</h3>\n<p>A user reports the home page is slow to load. Walk through your diagnostic approach: what data would you ask for, what would you investigate first?</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-5-tech-debt-trade-off\">Exercise 14.5 — Tech Debt Trade-off</h3>\n<p>Engineering is asking for 2 weeks to refactor the payments module. Write a memo to your VP arguing for or against, with reasoning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-6-privacy-review\">Exercise 14.6 — Privacy Review</h3>\n<p>For a hypothetical health-tracking app, list 10 privacy considerations from collection to deletion. What controls would users have?</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-7-mobile-platform-choice\">Exercise 14.7 — Mobile Platform Choice</h3>\n<p>For a new B2C fintech app launching in India, would you build native (separate iOS and Android), React Native, Flutter, or a PWA? Justify.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-14-8-read-a-real-design-doc\">Exercise 14.8 — Read a Real Design Doc</h3>\n<p>Find a public engineering design doc (search &quot;engineering design doc&quot; on GitHub or company blogs). Read it. Write a 1-page summary of the technical decisions and trade-offs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"14-23-references-for-part-14\">14.23 References for Part 14</h2>\n<p><strong>Books:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> — Martin Kleppmann. The most-recommended technical book for PMs and engineers. Dense but invaluable. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Designing-Data-Intensive-Applications-Reliable-Maintainable/dp/1449373321\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>The Pragmatic Programmer</em> — Hunt &amp; Thomas. Classic on engineering thinking. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Pragmatic-Programmer-journey-mastery-Anniversary/dp/0135957052\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>System Design Interview</em> — Alex Xu. Even non-interviewers benefit from these mental models. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/System-Design-Interview-insiders-Second/dp/B08CMF2CQF\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Designing Web APIs</em> — Jin et al. For PMs at API-led companies. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Designing-Web-APIs-Building-Developers/dp/1492026921\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>The Phoenix Project</em> — Gene Kim. Novel about DevOps; reveals operational reality. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Phoenix-Project-DevOps-Helping-Business/dp/0988262592\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> — Google's free book. (<a href=\"https://sre.google/books/\">sre.google</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Web Application Architecture</em> — Microsoft / industry resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Online resources:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>ByteByteGo (Alex Xu's blog and YouTube). Visual system design. (<a href=\"https://bytebytego.com\">bytebytego.com</a>)</li>\n<li>High Scalability blog. Real architecture stories. (<a href=\"http://highscalability.com\">highscalability.com</a>)</li>\n<li>Patrick Collison's tech-aware curiosity. (<a href=\"https://patrickcollison.com\">patrickcollison.com</a>)</li>\n<li>Stripe's API design philosophy posts. (<a href=\"https://stripe.com/blog\">stripe.com/blog</a>)</li>\n<li>AWS / GCP / Azure architecture centers. Free reference architectures.</li>\n<li>OWASP. (<a href=\"https://owasp.org\">owasp.org</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tutorials and courses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Database:</strong> Use the Mode SQL tutorial (Part 7). Then PostgreSQL official docs for depth.</li>\n<li><strong>APIs:</strong> Build a small project consuming Stripe's or Twilio's API. Read their docs.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud:</strong> AWS Cloud Practitioner certification (free study materials).</li>\n<li><strong>Security:</strong> OWASP top 10 walkthrough; Stanford's free CS155 lecture videos.</li>\n<li><strong>System design:</strong> ByteByteGo + system design mock interviews.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tools to learn:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Postman / Insomnia for API testing.</li>\n<li>DBeaver / TablePlus for database exploration.</li>\n<li>Chrome DevTools for web debugging.</li>\n<li>Sentry / Datadog for monitoring (read-only access).</li>\n<li>GitHub for code reading.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write SQL daily on Mode, DataLemur, StrataScratch.</li>\n<li>Read 1 engineering blog post per week (Stripe, Uber, Netflix engineering blogs).</li>\n<li>Skim 1 PR per week from your eng team.</li>\n<li>Try one cloud service free tier per month.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p><em>End of Part 14. The next part is the soul of this book in many ways: how to come up with great product ideas and build them from zero.</em></p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n");
